  "narration": {
    "title": "Bregman Booms, A Handful of Bats Cool Off",
    "intro": "Some weeks the leaderboard reshuffles; this one mostly just simmered. A cluster of Cubs bats caught fire while a few stars quietly slid a few points. Here's who moved.",
    "risers_lead": "Thomas Saggese was the loudest bat of the week, an 8-for-15 stretch with three homers for St. Louis and a 1.689 OPS. Alex Bregman kept the Cubs' good times rolling with five homers and 12 RBI over seven games, and rookie Carter Jensen chipped in four homers of his own in Kansas City.",
    "pfallers_lead": "Nothing dramatic on the mound this week. Jacob Misiorowski's form dipped from 95 to 97's neighborhood but he's still the class of the field at a 1.97 ERA, and the rest of the elite pitchers barely budged.",
    "hweek_lead": "Bregman's seven-game tear (12-for-31, five homers, 12 RBI) leads the hot list, and he's got company in fellow Cub Tyrone Taylor, who went 10-for-18. Roman Anthony added his own 10-for-22 for Boston.",
    "fallers_lead": "The cold snaps were mild but real: Alec Burleson went 1-for-22 and Munetaka Murakami managed just three hits in 24 tries, each shaving a couple dozen points off their season OPS. Shohei Ohtani's quiet week nudged him to a .906 OPS, still comfortably in the elite tier.",
    "colors_lead": "Only one tier change worth noting: Logan Webb slipped from above-average to average. A single step, not a collapse."
  }
}